	url = {https://www.sciencedirect.com/science/article/pii/B9780128212646000140},
	doi = {10.1016/B978-0-12-821264-6.00014-0},
	abstract = {Renewable energy resources are gradually replacing conventional fossil fuel sources for energy, which would eventually give rise to an energy platform with reduced environmental pollution and greenhouse gas emissions. Hydrogen (H2) is recognized as an environment-friendly, renewable, clean energy, with a combustion energy yield greater than that of hydrocarbon fuels. Moreover, it completely avoids the emission of air pollutants, such as {NOx}, {SOx}, and greenhouse gases. The conventional processes of hydrogen production from fossil and water involve enormous energy consumption and greenhouse gas emissions. On the other hand, biohydrogen production from renewable sources has significant advantages in terms of the process cost and sustainability. Among the different biohydrogen production technologies, continuous systems support scale-up and commercialization. This chapter reviews the various continuous bioreactor configurations such as continuous stirred tank reactor, upflow anaerobic sludge blanket reactor, packed bed reactor, gas lift reactor, membrane bioreactor, fluidized bed reactor, anaerobic baffled reactor, among others, and their operational strategies for biohydrogen production. Also, key factors affecting reactor performance and strategies for improving the process efficiency are discussed in detail.},
